# frozen_string_literal: true

module Ai
  # Prompt construction (AD-16/AD-5, epic P2-5 ticket 03): every AI surface's
  # messages are built HERE, from server-assembled context — the model never
  # sees client prose as instructions. The system prompt carries the
  # materials-sourcing law (NFR-1) and the JSON-only reply contract; each
  # surface's user prompt carries the assembled context and its exact reply
  # schema. The reply language (zh/en) is a prompt parameter on every
  # builder — one parameter, one policy (AD-16) — and its directive line is
  # generated by the ONE language_directive helper (epic-34 T1), never
  # hand-written per prompt.
  module Prompts
    SYSTEM = <<~PROMPT.freeze
      You are Synapse's study AI. You answer strictly from the provided study
      material context — you never invent content that is not grounded in it.
      Guardrails: you are a study companion, hint-first, and you say so
      honestly when the material does not cover a request. You reply ONLY
      with the single JSON object the task specifies — no prose, no code
      fences.
    PROMPT

    QUESTION_TARGET = 5

    # The note surface's word target (FR-40, epic P2-12 T1): a single constant
    # mirroring QUESTION_TARGET's posture, with the prompt's honest floor and
    # ceiling derived from it (the ~800–1200 words band the delta pins).
    NOTE_TARGET = 1_000
    NOTE_MIN_WORDS = NOTE_TARGET - 200
    NOTE_MAX_WORDS = NOTE_TARGET + 200

    # The enrichment marker contract (delta decision 1 — markers-only v1):
    # kinds the model may place, and the EXACT line form the note view parses
    # into placeholder cards and export preserves.
    NOTE_MARKER_KINDS = %w[diagram chart image].freeze

    # The Study Focus item target (FR-17, epic-13 T4): a single constant
    # mirroring QUESTION_TARGET/NOTE_TARGET's posture — the prompt asks for
    # about this many core items; an honest count may land below it (items
    # the server's index map drops never come back invented).
    FOCUS_TARGET = 5

    # The companion quiz-block rule (epic-24 T4): shared by companion and
    # plan_companion — a quiz request ends in ONE fenced ```synapse-quiz JSON
    # block riding the reply TEXT (the client renders it interactive; the SSE
    # wire + thread persistence stay byte-frozen). The answer never leaves the
    # block (NFR-8's server half), and "keywords" grades the short form the
    # same way the open-answer grader counts terms.
    QUIZ_RULE = <<~RULE.freeze
      If their question asks you to test them (考考我 / quiz me / test me), TEST them:
      end your reply with EXACTLY ONE fenced code block tagged synapse-quiz holding ONE question
      sourced ONLY from the material above, with the JSON keys EXACTLY as shown —
      choice form: {"kind":"choice","question":"…","options":["…","…","…","…"],"answer":0,"explanation":"…"}
      short form: {"kind":"short","question":"…","answerText":"…","keywords":["…","…"],"explanation":"…"}
      ("answer" is the 0-based index of the correct option; "keywords" are 2–3 terms a good short
      answer must use.) Write the question in the reply language. One short lead-in line may
      precede the block — never reveal the answer or explanation outside the block.
      The fence is MANDATORY: never write the JSON as bare text in the reply — a payload not
      wrapped in the synapse-quiz fence is a protocol violation, even when the JSON itself is perfect.
    RULE

    # The proposal's materials-block budget (epic-27): the proposal reads the
    # materials' shape, not their full text — per-material and total slices
    # at prompt assembly (the command's own 20k/material bound is the honest
    # storage cap; this is the tighter prompt cap). Module-level constants —
    # the specs read them as Ai::Prompts::MAX_PROMPT_MATERIAL_*.
    MAX_PROMPT_MATERIAL_CHARS = 8_000
    MAX_PROMPT_MATERIAL_TOTAL = 30_000

    # The proposal's system posture (2026-11-10): the shared SYSTEM's
    # "reply ONLY with the single JSON object" contract CONFLICTS with the
    # proposal's tag format — a model resolving the conflict sometimes drops
    # the wrapper tags entirely (the live no-<outline> failure). The
    # proposal speaks its own system message: the tags ARE the reply
    # contract, the JSON is strict, and content quotes are escaped.
    PROPOSAL_SYSTEM = <<~PROMPT.freeze
      You are Synapse's study AI. You design study plans grounded in the
      learner's message and the attached materials — you never invent
      content that is not grounded in them. Guardrails: you are a study
      companion; when the materials do not cover the request, say so inside
      the outline honestly. Your reply is EXACTLY the two-tag format the
      task specifies — <outline>…</outline> immediately followed by
      <proposal>…</proposal>, nothing before, between, or after, and no
      code fences anywhere. The proposal payload is ONE strict JSON object:
      any double quote INSIDE a string value must be escaped with a
      backslash — never emit a raw quote inside a string.
    PROMPT

    class << self
      # Quiz generation (ticket 03; targeting epic P2-10 T5; material scope
      # epic-13 T2): node-attributed (AD-6) + materials-sourced (NFR-1)
      # questions in the scope the command assembled. `quota_plan` is T5's
      # weak-spot selection bias (FR-21): the server-computed per-node
      # question quotas — a selection/weighting layer ONLY, never a new
      # source; sections still cite the assembled context. nil keeps the
      # prompt byte-for-byte identical to the pre-T5 shape. `attribution_note`
      # is T2's material-scope map: the confirmed section→node pairs, the
      # ONLY allowed question sources — nil keeps the note slot empty (the
      # pre-T2 shape).
      def quiz(mode:, language:, context:, question_target: QUESTION_TARGET, quota_plan: nil, attribution_note: nil)
        [ system,
          user(<<~PROMPT)
            Study material context (the ONLY allowed source):
            ---
            #{context.text}
            ---
            #{context_note(context)}#{quota_note(quota_plan)}#{attribution_map_note(attribution_note)}

            Generate exactly #{question_target} quiz questions from ONLY the material above.
            Mode "#{mode}": #{mode_rule(mode)}
            Every question MUST carry:
            - "nodeId": one of the [node id=…] values above (attribution, AD-6).
            - "kind": "open" or "choice".
            - "prompt", "explanation": the question and its teaching explanation.
            - "sourceMaterialId" + "sourceSectionId": the [material id=…] and [section id=…] the question's answer is grounded in (provenance, FR-18).
            - "keywords": the key terms a correct answer must use (open-answer grading input).
            Choice questions additionally carry "options" (2–4 strings) and "correctOptionIndex" (0-based Integer).
            #{language_directive(language)}

            Reply ONLY with JSON: {"questions":[{"nodeId":"…","kind":"open","prompt":"…","options":null,"correctOptionIndex":null,"sourceMaterialId":"…","sourceSectionId":"…","keywords":["…"],"explanation":"…"}]}
          PROMPT
        ]
      end

      # Flashcard generation (FR-19's one-card-per-section shape): a deck for
      # ONE material's sections.
      def flashcards(language:, context:)
        [ system,
          user(<<~PROMPT)
            Study material context (the ONLY allowed source):
            ---
            #{context.text}
            ---
            #{context_note(context)}

            Generate exactly one flashcard per [section id=…] above: "front" is a question or cue for that section's core idea, "back" is its answer.
            #{language_directive(language)}

            Reply ONLY with JSON: {"cards":[{"front":"…","back":"…"}]}
          PROMPT
        ]
      end

      # Section→node mapping (FR-45, epic P2-10 T1): a material's sections
      # against the plan's existing nodes — map onto node ids, propose new
      # node titles for coverage gaps, and leave truly out-of-scope sections
      # with nulls (the command turns those into explicit Unmapped rows —
      # nothing is silently dropped, the model just declines to place them).
      def mapping(language:, context:)
        [ system,
          user(<<~PROMPT)
            Study material context (the ONLY allowed source):
            ---
            #{context.text}
            ---
            #{context_note(context)}

            Map EVERY [section id=…] above onto the plan's [node id=…] entries. For each section return exactly one entry:
            - The section teaches an existing node's topic → "nodeId" is that node's exact id, "proposedNodeTitle" null.
            - NO existing node covers the section → "proposedNodeTitle" is a short new node title (2–8 words); reuse the SAME title for every section that belongs to one new node. "nodeId" null.
            - The section does not belong to this plan's scope at all → both null.
            Every section id in the index MUST appear exactly once in "mappings" — never drop a section.
            #{language_directive(language, "every proposedNodeTitle")}

            Reply ONLY with JSON: {"mappings":[{"sectionId":"…","nodeId":"…","proposedNodeTitle":null}]}
          PROMPT
        ]
      end

      # Note generation (FR-40, epic P2-12 T1; delta decisions 1+5): a
      # standalone, exportable Markdown study note assembled from ONLY the
      # context the command gathered (NFR-1). The reply is the Markdown note
      # itself — NOT JSON (the system prompt's JSON contract is overridden by
      # this task's own reply contract, the same way the proposal's tag
      # contract overrides it). Visual enrichment rides as PLACEMENT markers
      # (delta decision 1's markers-only v1): the model decides WHERE a visual
      # aids understanding and emits [[enrich|kind|suggestion]] on its own
      # line; content stays text and no assets are stored in v1.
      # `quota_plan` is T2's weak-spot coverage bias (FR-22, epic P2-12): the
      # server-computed per-node coverage weights — a selection/weighting
      # layer ONLY (FR-21's targeting layer), never a new source; content
      # still comes only from the assembled context. nil keeps the prompt
      # byte-for-byte identical to the T1 shape (pinned full-string-eq in the
      # note command spec).
      def note(language:, context:, quota_plan: nil)
        [ system,
          user(<<~PROMPT)
            Study material context (the ONLY allowed source):
            ---
            #{context.text}
            ---
            #{context_note(context)}#{coverage_note(quota_plan)}

            Write a standalone, exportable Markdown study note from ONLY the material above — a learner must be able to read it without the original material at hand.
            Begin the note with a single level-1 heading ("# …") that names the note; structure it with headings and short paragraphs.
            Aim for #{NOTE_MIN_WORDS}–#{NOTE_MAX_WORDS} words (target: #{NOTE_TARGET}).
            Where a visual would genuinely aid understanding, insert an enrichment marker on its own line, in EXACTLY this form:
            [[enrich|kind|what the visual should show]]
            kind is one of: #{NOTE_MARKER_KINDS.map { |kind| "\"#{kind}\"" }.join(", ")}. Placement is your call — only where a picture truly helps; never place a marker for something the material does not ground.
            #{language_directive(language)}

            Reply ONLY with the Markdown note itself — no code fences, no commentary before or after.
          PROMPT
        ]
      end

      # Study Focus generation (FR-17, epic-13 T4; OQ-1): the learner's own
      # annotations distilled into core study items. The annotations ride
      # the context FIRST (numbered [annotation N] lines — the reply's
      # sourceAnnotationIndex vocabulary); the section index follows for
      # coherence ONLY. The NFR-1 law is doubled down here: items distill
      # from ONLY the annotations — the index is never a content source.
      # The command maps every sourceAnnotationIndex to that annotation's
      # range jsonb server-side (an out-of-range index drops the item — an
      # honest count landing below the target).
      def study_focus(language:, context:)
        [ system,
          user(<<~PROMPT)
            The learner's annotations on ONE study material (the ONLY allowed source):
            ---
            #{context.text}
            ---
            #{context_note(context)}

            Distill these annotations into about #{FOCUS_TARGET} core study items — the points a learner must take away from what they marked.
            Every item MUST carry:
            - "title": a short item name (a few words, grounded in the annotations).
            - "detail": one or two sentences explaining the point, distilled from its annotation.
            - "sourceAnnotationIndex": the [annotation N] the item distills (the exact Integer N).
            Distill from ONLY the annotations above — never invent content an annotation does not ground; the section index is for orientation, not sourcing.
            #{language_directive(language)}

            Reply ONLY with JSON: {"items":[{"title":"…","detail":"…","sourceAnnotationIndex":1}]}
          PROMPT
        ]
      end

      # The generation-backed Today suggestion (epic-18 T2, spec decisions
      # 7–11): pick ONE "study next" node from the SERVER-assembled candidate
      # pool (the plan's non-mastered, confirmed-mapped nodes, weakest first,
      # each with its server-computed mastery facts + open-mistake count) and
      # say why in one line. Title-only leak discipline: the candidate index
      # rides ids + titles + server-computed state ONLY — no section text, no
      # material content — so the reason can only be grounded in the facts the
      # server gave (the command validates the reply's nodeId against the same
      # pool; an unresolvable id is an honest INTERNAL, never a fabricated
      # fallback).
      def today_suggestion(language:, candidates:)
        index_lines = candidates.map do |candidate|
          "[node id=#{candidate[:node].id}] #{candidate[:node].title} — mastery #{candidate[:score]}/100, state #{candidate[:state]}, open mistakes #{candidate[:open_mistakes]}"
        end
        [ system,
          user(<<~PROMPT)
            The learner's study-plan nodes with server-computed state (the ONLY allowed targets):
            ---
            #{index_lines.join("\n")}
            ---

            Pick EXACTLY ONE node the learner should study next and explain why in ONE line.
            - "nodeId": the chosen node's id EXACTLY as written in the [node id=…] index above — never invent or alter an id.
            - "nodeTitle": that node's title EXACTLY as written above.
            - "reason": ONE short sentence grounded ONLY in the server-computed facts above (the mastery score, the state, the open-mistake count) — never invent claims about what the node's material contains.
            The weakest, least-covered nodes come FIRST in the index — prefer them.
            #{language_directive(language, '"reason"')}

            Reply ONLY with JSON: {"nodeId":"…","nodeTitle":"…","reason":"…"}
          PROMPT
        ]
      end

      # Open-answer grading (AD-5): grade + rationale against the section the
      # question cites. The keyword-count rule is Phase-1's documented
      # simulated-adapter rule (≥2 hits correct, 1 partial, 0 wrong), passed
      # to the real model verbatim.
      def grade(answer:, keywords:, source_text:, language:)
        [ system,
          user(<<~PROMPT)
            Grade a learner's open answer against its source section.
            Source section (the grading ground truth):
            ---
            #{source_text}
            ---
            Expected key terms: #{keywords.inspect}

            Learner's answer:
            ---
            #{answer}
            ---

            Grade by how many expected key terms the answer correctly uses: 0 terms → "wrong", 1 term → "partial", 2 or more → "correct".
            "rationale": a short teaching explanation of the grade.
            "quote": the exact sentence from the source section that best supports the grade (or null if none applies).
            #{language_directive(language, "your rationale")}

            Reply ONLY with JSON: {"outcome":"correct|partial|wrong","rationale":"…","quote":"…"}
          PROMPT
        ]
      end

      # Companion chat (FR-11/12, AD-5): single-turn, grounded on the
      # ReadingPosition the ticket carried. The reply is plain prose — every
      # character streams as token events. The guardrail posture (rung) is
      # REQUEST-derived server-side (escalated → "answer", else "hint") —
      # the ladder rule rides the contract, never the model's mood.
      # Epic-24 T4: a quiz request (考考我 / quiz me / test me) ends in ONE
      # fenced ```synapse-quiz JSON block (the client renders it as an
      # interactive question; the block rides the reply TEXT so the SSE wire
      # and the thread persistence stay byte-frozen). The answer lives ONLY
      # inside the block — never in the lead prose (NFR-8's server half).
      def companion(question:, position_text:, language:, escalated:, context:)
        [ system,
          user(<<~PROMPT)
            The learner is reading a material. Study material context (the ONLY allowed source):
            ---
            #{context.text}
            ---
            #{context_note(context)}
            Their current reading position text:
            ---
            #{position_text}
            ---

            Their question: #{question}

            #{escalated ? "The learner explicitly asked for the direct answer — give it, grounded and cited." : "Stay hint-first: guide toward the answer rather than dumping it (the ladder rule — the learner can escalate with one click)."}
            #{QUIZ_RULE}
            Answer ONLY from the material above; if it does not cover the question, say so honestly.
            #{language_directive(language, "your entire reply")}
            Reply in flowing prose — never JSON, never code fences; the learner reads your words as a chat message.
          PROMPT
        ]
      end

      # Plan-level companion chat (FR-46, epic P2-12 T3): the companion
      # prompt shape with the plan grounding substituted — no reading-position
      # section (the plan scope carries no ReadingPosition), and an inline
      # citation instruction instead (answers name the material/section they
      # rest on — FR-46's citations without a document). Always hint-first:
      # the plan_companion payload has no escalation flag (delta decision 6),
      # so the ladder input never crosses the ticket.
      def plan_companion(question:, language:, context:)
        [ system,
          user(<<~PROMPT)
            The learner is asking about their whole study plan. Study material context (the ONLY allowed source):
            ---
            #{context.text}
            ---
            #{context_note(context)}
            When an answer rests on specific parts, cite them inline by the material and section names from the context (e.g. 「《…》的「…」一节」 / "the section '…' of '…'").

            Their question: #{question}

            Stay hint-first: guide toward the answer rather than dumping it (the ladder rule).
            #{QUIZ_RULE}
            Answer ONLY from the material above; if it does not cover the question, say so honestly.
            #{language_directive(language, "your entire reply")}
            Reply in flowing prose — never JSON, never code fences; the learner reads your words as a chat message.
          PROMPT
        ]
      end

      # Plan-creation proposal (FR-1/AD-5): topic (+ optional goal) → a
      # staged roadmap proposal. The reply format is the frozen tag contract
      # (ticket 04): a readable outline between the outline tags (what the
      # client streams as tokens) and the strict proposal JSON between the
      # proposal tags (what the terminal event carries — the client's
      # follow-up confirm consumes it verbatim). epic-16 T1 upgraded the
      # JSON: stages carry full nodes ({title, description, optional}) and
      # top-level title-paired prerequisite edges, under four pinned
      # constraints — at most 16 nodes (the scale cap, grill decision 3),
      # unique titles (edges address nodes by title), forward-only edges
      # (the from node's stage strictly before the to node's — cycles
      # impossible by construction), and an inbound edge for every node
      # outside the first stage. Nothing downstream trusts the constraints:
      # unresolvable/invalid edges drop at confirm (silent-but-logged).
      #
      # The materials block is the proposal's grounding (epic-27): the
      # MESSAGE drives the plan's goal, the MATERIALS ground what the plan
      # covers — deliberately NOT the quiz prompt's "ONLY allowed source"
      # law (a plan's structure is naming, not extraction). Sliced here —
      # the proposal reads the materials' shape, not their full text.
      def proposal(message:, materials:, language:)
        material_block = proposal_material_block(materials)
        # Files-only creation (2026-11-10): a blank message is honest inputs —
        # the materials alone state the ground to cover.
        message_line =
          if message.to_s.strip.empty?
            "The learner attached study materials without a message — design the plan from ONLY what the materials cover."
          else
            "The learner's message: #{message}"
          end
        [ { role: "system", content: PROPOSAL_SYSTEM },
          user(<<~PROMPT)
            Design a study plan. #{message_line}
            #{material_block}
            Reply in EXACTLY this format — nothing before, between, or after the tags:
            <outline>
            A short readable outline of the plan: one line per stage ("第 1 阶段：…" / "Stage 1: …"), then its node titles as "- …" lines.
            </outline>
            <proposal>
            {"topic":"A short plan title derived from the message","stages":[{"name":"…","nodes":[{"title":"…","description":"…","optional":false}]}],"edges":[["…","…"]]}
            </proposal>

            2–5 stages, at most 16 nodes total, ordered from foundations to practice.
            Every node carries "description" (one short line saying what the node covers) and "optional" (true only for a node the learner may skip without losing the thread; otherwise false).
            "edges" are prerequisite pairs [fromTitle, toTitle] — address each node by its title EXACTLY as written (titles are unique), and the from node's stage must come strictly BEFORE the to node's stage (forward-only). Give every node outside the first stage at least one inbound edge.
            When study material context is present above, tailor the stages/nodes/descriptions to what the materials actually cover (the message states the goal; the materials state the ground to cover) — never pad the plan with topics the materials do not touch.
            #{language_directive(language, "the outline lines AND the JSON's strings")}
          PROMPT
        ]
      end

      private

      def proposal_material_block(materials)
        list = (materials || []).filter_map do |material|
          text = material["text"].to_s.slice(0, MAX_PROMPT_MATERIAL_CHARS)
          next if text.strip.empty?
          "— #{material['title']} (#{material['kind']}):\n#{text}"
        end
        return "" if list.empty?
        joined = list.join("\n\n")[0, MAX_PROMPT_MATERIAL_TOTAL]
        "\nStudy material context (the attached materials — tailor the plan to what they cover):\n#{joined}\n"
      end

      def system
        { role: "system", content: SYSTEM }
      end

      def user(content)
        { role: "user", content: content }
      end

      # The language-directive single point (epic-34 T1): every builder's
      # reply-language line is generated HERE — one formatter, one policy
      # (the reply language is a prompt parameter on every builder, AD-16;
      # no builder hand-writes its own language line anymore). `scope` names
      # what must be written in the reply language; the default covers the
      # dominant whole-reply form.
      def language_directive(language, scope = "every user-facing string")
        "Write #{scope} in #{language_word(language)}."
      end

      def language_word(language)
        language == "zh" ? "简体中文" : "English"
      end

      def mode_rule(mode)
        case mode
        when "open" then "every question is kind \"open\" (free-text answer)."
        when "choice" then "every question is kind \"choice\" (options + correctOptionIndex)."
        when "mix" then "include BOTH kinds \"open\" and \"choice\"."
        end
      end

      def context_note(context)
        context.truncated ? "Note: the context above is PARTIAL (truncated to the budget) — cite only sections you can see." : ""
      end

      # T5's weak-spot quota block (FR-21): the deterministic server-side
      # allocation the command computed (Ai::Targeting). Blank → nothing —
      # the pre-T5 prompt shape, byte-for-byte.
      def quota_note(quota_plan)
        return "" if quota_plan.blank?

        <<~NOTE

          Weak-spot quota plan (server-computed from the learner's Mastery Scores and mistake log — distribute EXACTLY):
          #{quota_plan}
          These quotas bias question selection ONLY — every question still cites its [material id=…] + [section id=…] provenance from ONLY the context above (FR-18); never invent content a quota cannot ground.
        NOTE
      end

      # T2's material-scope attribution block (epic-13 T2, FR-14): the
      # confirmed section→node pairs the command resolved — the ONLY allowed
      # question sources, with the map owning attribution server-side. The
      # command passes the pre-rendered pair lines; nil → nothing (the
      # pre-T2 prompt shape, byte-for-byte).
      def attribution_map_note(pairs_lines)
        return "" if pairs_lines.blank?

        <<~NOTE

          Section→node attribution map (server-computed from the learner's CONFIRMED section mappings — these pairs are the ONLY allowed question sources):
          #{pairs_lines.join("\n")}
          Source every question from a mapped section above and set "nodeId" to that section's mapped node id EXACTLY (the map owns attribution); a section absent from the map must not produce any question.
        NOTE
      end

      # T2's weak-spot coverage block (FR-22, epic P2-12): the deterministic
      # server-side allocation the command computed (Ai::Targeting over the
      # SHARED Ai::TargetingCandidates builder), in the quiz quota table's
      # line shape repurposed for note coverage. Blank → nothing — the T1
      # prompt shape, byte-for-byte (pinned full-string-eq in the note
      # command spec).
      def coverage_note(quota_plan)
        return "" if quota_plan.blank?

        <<~NOTE

          Weak-spot coverage plan (server-computed from the learner's Mastery Scores and mistake log — cover the marked nodes EXACTLY as allotted):
          #{quota_plan}
          This note is aimed at what the learner has not yet demonstrated — cover the marked nodes proportionally to their gaps.
          Content comes from ONLY the context above (NFR-1) — never invent content a quota cannot ground.
        NOTE
      end
    end
  end
end
